              "id": "glog-4208c57d-b43e-4f82-88ea-3f6ed969f78e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source buffer is larger than the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, the `memcpy` function is copying 4 bytes from `buf1[12]` to `pmmetrics_version`. If `pmmetrics_version` is not large enough to hold 4 bytes, this can result in a buffer overflow vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`. Alternatively, you can use functions that do check the size of the destination buffer, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif (sizeof(pmmetrics_version) >= 4) {\n    memcpy(&pmmetrics_version, &buf1[12], 4);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, the size of `pmmetrics_version` is checked before calling `memcpy`. If `pmmetrics_version` is not large enough to hold 4 bytes, an error is handled.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so the only library dependency required by the code example is:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-efa0467c-5804-42fd-9ef2-4a09b985cfbf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is considered unsafe because it does not guarantee that a null character will be appended at the end of the destination string. This can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is longer than the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, the `strncpy` function is used to copy a string from `empty.c_str()` to `market_name`, with a maximum length of `AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1`. If the length of `empty.c_str()` is greater than `AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1`, the `market_name` string will not be null-terminated, which can lead to undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use safer string copy functions that guarantee null-termination of the destination string. In C++, you can use the `std::string` class and its associated functions to safely manipulate strings.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncpy` function with the `std::string::copy` function, which ensures null-termination of the destination string:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string empty;\n// ...\nstd::string market_name = empty.substr(0, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-25cbce3f-d6e9-4fbc-abc2-91d2899582a7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is considered unsafe because it does not guarantee null-termination of the destination string when the source string length is greater than or equal to the destination buffer size. This can lead to buffer overflows, which can be exploited to execute arbitrary code, overwrite data, or cause a system crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination of the destination string. In C++, the `std::string` class provides safe string handling functionality. Avoid using C-style string handling functions like `strncpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncpy` function with the `std::string::copy` function, which ensures null-termination of the destination string:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string version;\nversion = empty.substr(0, len-1);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
